# 📘 Mathematics for Information Security

### From Group Theory to Post-Quantum Cryptography

**Author:** Haijian Shao

## 📖 Overview

This repository provides a **mathematical lecture note system for information security and cryptography**, designed for undergraduate and graduate students, as well as researchers and engineers.

The content systematically covers the **core mathematical foundations of modern cryptography**, bridging **rigorous theory and engineering practice**. Instead of treating mathematics as abstract formalism, this project organizes mathematical structures around **real cryptographic problems and implementations**.

📌 **Status:**
This project is under continuous development. The full plan includes **10 chapters**, with several already released and more being iteratively added.

## 🎯 Objectives

* Build a **derivable and applicable mathematical toolkit** for cryptography
* Explain **why cryptographic algorithms work**, not just how
* Connect **algebraic structures → security assumptions → real systems**
* Support **research, engineering, and post-quantum cryptography learning**

---

## 📚 Table of Contents

### Part I: Discrete Algebraic Structures (Foundations of Classical Cryptography)

#### 1. Number Theory

* Congruences and modular arithmetic
* GCD / LCM (Euclidean & Extended Euclidean Algorithm)
* Bézout’s theorem (modular inverse foundation)
* Prime generation and fundamental theorem of arithmetic

#### 2. Group Theory

* Group axioms and cryptographic constraints
* Abelian vs non-Abelian groups
* Subgroups, normal subgroups, quotient groups
* Homomorphisms and isomorphisms

#### 3. Ring Theory and Ideals

* Ring structures and algebraic properties
* Residue class rings ( \mathbb{Z}_m )
* Ideals, principal ideals, maximal ideals
* Quotient rings and finite field construction

#### 4. Primitive Roots & Discrete Logarithms

* Cyclic group structures
* Generator selection and safe primes
* Discrete Logarithm Problem (DLP)
* Practical tools (GMP, OpenSSL)

---

### Part II: Finite Fields and Constructions (Engineering Foundations)

#### 5. Finite Fields

* Prime fields ( \mathbb{F}*p ) and extensions ( \mathbb{F}*{p^n} )
* Irreducible polynomials
* Field arithmetic implementation
* Mapping ( \mathbb{Z}_2 \rightarrow \text{GF}(2^8) )

#### 6. Applications of Finite Fields

* AES/SM4 S-box construction
* Polynomial multiplication optimization
* Error correction codes (CRC/ECC)
* Hardware/software optimization

---

### Part III: Elliptic Curve Cryptography (Efficient Public-Key Systems)

#### 7. Algebra of Elliptic Curves

* Group law (point addition & multiplication)
* Curves over finite fields
* ECDLP hardness and security

#### 8. ECC / SM2 Engineering

* SM2 parameter standards
* Coordinate optimization (affine/projective)
* Side-channel resistance
* ECC vs RSA comparison

---

### Part IV: Quantum Threats & Post-Quantum Cryptography

#### 9. Quantum Risks

* Shor’s algorithm
* Impact on RSA/ECC
* Security boundary shifts

#### 10. Lattice-Based Cryptography

* Lattice fundamentals (basis, volume)
* SVP / CVP problems
* LWE / RLWE
* Standards: Kyber, Dilithium

---

### Part V: Engineering Practice & Pitfalls

* Algebra behind RSA / DH / SM2
* Subgroup optimization in ECC
* Ideals and quotient groups in cryptography
* Small subgroup attacks
* Big integer overflow issues
* Post-quantum engineering trade-offs

---

## 🚀 Key Features

* 🔗 **Theory + Engineering Integration**
  Every mathematical concept is tied to a real cryptographic use case

* 🧠 **Problem-Driven Structure**
  Mathematics is introduced through security problems

* 🧪 **Example-Based Learning**
  Includes concrete computations:

  * Modular groups
  * AES S-box construction
  * ECC point operations

* 📊 **Visualization Support**
  TikZ diagrams for:

  * GCD geometry
  * Cosets
  * Lattice structures

* 🏭 **Industry-Oriented**
  Covers real-world standards:

  * TLS / SSH
  * SM2
  * Post-quantum cryptography

---

## 🧭 Learning Paths

### 1. Classical Cryptography

```
Congruence → Group → Primitive Root → DLP → ECC
```

### 2. Engineering Implementation

```
Finite Field → Field Extension → ECC → SM2
```

### 3. Post-Quantum Cryptography

```
Linear Algebra → Lattice → SVP/CVP → LWE/RLWE
```

